---
import {METADATA, LINKS, CAREER} from "../lib/constants";

interface Props {
    title: string;
    description: string;
}

const {title, description} = Astro.props;

/*
 * The body's height floor below is a MINIMUM, deliberately, and the distinction
 * is load-bearing. It used to be an exact viewport height from the medium
 * breakpoint up, which locked the page to 100vh a full breakpoint below where
 * the single-screen bento layout is actually defined — that contract lives on
 * <main> in index.astro and is written against the large breakpoint only.
 *
 * Between 768px and 1023px the grid is just two columns, so the same content is
 * roughly 1160px tall. Against an exact 100vh body the implicit `auto` rows were
 * compressed to fit the viewport and every card's own clipping ate the
 * remainder: at 768x900 four of the eight cards lost content — the intro card
 * 98.45px, its entire row of controls; the Now card 9.23px, the final line of its
 * copy; and each goal card 5.86px, the "last year's" line. Two cards from 800 to
 * 1023px, worst 76.13px down to 62.58px, and one at 768x1024 losing 36.61px.
 * Measured against each card's padding box, which is what it clips to.
 *
 * Nothing scrolled, because the body was exactly as tall as the viewport, so the
 * loss was silent and unrecoverable rather than merely below the fold.
 *
 * A floor fixes that: the body still stretches to the full viewport, so the
 * centring below still centres, and <main> is still capped at the large
 * breakpoint. Where content genuinely needs more room the page now grows and
 * scrolls instead of clipping. Keep this a floor — restoring an exact height
 * reintroduces the defect at every viewport shorter than the content.
 *
 * "Grows instead of clipping" is about the PAGE, and it used to come with a caveat:
 * a fixed max-height on a box INSIDE a card still turns growth into clipping
 * however tall the page is allowed to become. The intro card's copy column carried
 * such a cap, and under text-only zoom at the narrowest medium width it still bit —
 * at 768x900 the cards clipping went 4 -> 0 at a 16px root and 6 -> 0 at 20px, but
 * at a 24px root one card still lost 48px of its control row, against seven cards
 * and 241.5px before. Better by an order of magnitude, not zero.
 *
 * That residue is closed now, and closed at its source rather than here. `main`'s
 * height budget and every breakpoint are font-relative (index.astro and
 * uno.config.ts), so the grid grows with the reader's text and this floor lets the
 * result scroll; the copy column's cap went font-relative in the same pass, and
 * `tests/card-fill.test.ts` now forbids an absolute one anywhere inside a card.
 * Swept as ink past each card's BOTTOM clip edge: 0 at every viewport from 320 to 2560
 * wide, at every root size from 16px to 40px. Name the edge — the right edge was NOT zero
 * when that sweep was published, and it took its own change to close (the control-row
 * note further down this file, and index.astro). A card grows vertically with its content
 * and never horizontally, which is the whole reason the two edges needed separate work.
 *
 * It also fixes the large breakpoint, which the exact height broke as well, and
 * an earlier version of this comment wrongly claimed it left untouched. <main>
 * carries a minimum height of its own at that breakpoint (index.astro owns the
 * number — do not copy it here, it moves), so on a viewport shorter than that
 * minimum the exact-height body could not contain it; because the body centres
 * its children, the overflow was split top and bottom and the top half sat above
 * the scroll origin, where no scrolling reaches. Measured on production against
 * the value in force then: 94px of the first card unreachable at 1024x500, 69 at
 * 550, 44 at 600, 19 at 650, none from 700 up, and the same at every width from
 * 1024 to 1920 — a function of height alone. A laptop-sized bug, not an edge
 * case. Zero unreachable at all of those now.
 * At or above that minimum the large-breakpoint geometry is unchanged, rect for
 * rect.
 *
 * tests/page-fit.test.ts pins it. Utility names are described rather than
 * spelled here on purpose: UnoCSS scans this file, so writing one would emit a
 * real CSS rule that no element wears.
 */
const image = `${Astro.url.origin}/preview.jpg`;

const schema = {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Person",
    name: METADATA.name,
    url: METADATA.site_url,
    sameAs: LINKS.filter(item => /^https?:\/\//.test(item.link)).map(item => item.link),
    image: METADATA.image_url,
    jobTitle: CAREER[0].job_name,
    worksFor: {
        "@type": "Organization", name: CAREER[0].company, address: {
            "@type": "PostalAddress",
            addressLocality: METADATA.address_locality,
            addressCountry: METADATA.address_country,
        },
    },
    nationality: {
        "@type": "Country", name: METADATA.address_locality,
    },
};
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/>
    <link rel="icon" type="image/x-icon" href="/favicon.ico"/>
    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator}/>
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description}/>
    <meta name="robots" content="index, follow"/>
    <!-- Basic OG tags for sharing your website's content on platforms like Facebook and LinkedIn -->
    <meta property="og:title" content={title}/>
    <meta property="og:description" content={description}/>
    <meta property="og:type" content="website"/>
    <meta property="og:url" content={Astro.url.origin}/>
    <meta
            property="og:image"
            content={image}
    />
    <!-- Basic Twitter Card tags -->
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/>
    <meta name="twitter:title" content={title}/>
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description}/>
    <meta
            name="twitter:image"
            content={image}
    />
    <link href={Astro.url.href} rel="canonical"/>
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)}/>
    <script defer src="https://cloud.umami.is/script.js" data-website-id={import.meta.env.UMAMI_ID}></script>
    <link rel="sitemap" href="/sitemap-index.xml"/>
    <script is:inline>
        (() => {
            const stored = localStorage.getItem("theme");
            const theme = stored === "light" || stored === "dark"
                ? stored
                : (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches ? "dark" : "light");
            document.documentElement.dataset.theme = theme;
        })();
    </script>
</head>
<body
        class="bg-[var(--background)] md:min-h-screen flex flex-col justify-center items-center"
>
<slot/>
</body>
</html>

<style is:global>
    body {
        margin: 0;
        font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, "Oxygen", Ubuntu, Cantarell, "Fira Sans", "Droid Sans", Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
        -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
        background-color: var(--background);
        color: var(--text);
        transition: background-color 0.3s ease, color 0.3s ease;
    }

    b {
        font-weight: 700 !important;
    }

    /* Theme Variables
     *
     * Seven roles, deliberately separate tokens even where several of them resolve
     * to the same hex today:
     *
     *   --shadow          the offset plate cast by the portrait and the controls
     *   --accent          the interactive affordance: control border, hover ink
     *   --progress-fill   the *marked* region of a progress bar
     *   --progress-track  the unmarked remainder of that bar
     *   --status-live     the Now card's live indicator dot
     *   --status-halo     that dot's decorative pulsing halo
     *   --brand-ink       a brand-coloured glyph standing in for a word in prose
     *
     * --brand-ink is worn by exactly one thing, the footer heart, and it is NOT
     * --accent even though the two match in light mode. --accent is the
     * interactive affordance, and nothing about that heart responds to a pointer
     * or a key; re-toning what a control does on hover must not re-tone a word in
     * a sentence. That is the same coupling --progress-fill was split out to
     * break, so reaching for --accent here would rebuild it under a new name.
     * It is not --status-live either: that reports a live state, and a heart in
     * "Built with <heart>" reports nothing — it IS the word, which is why the
     * span beside it supplies "love" for a screen reader.
     *
     * Being a word rather than an ornament is also why it is toned as ink and
     * measured as text: 6.52:1 on the light card, 9.08:1 on the dark one, both
     * clear of the 4.5:1 that 12px prose asks for. And it follows the surface the
     * way --progress-fill does, deep on light and pale on dark, so the mark stays
     * a shade of the same brand red in both themes instead of inverting into a
     * different colour.
     *
     * The bar used to paint --shadow, so re-toning the plate silently re-toned
     * the data. Two colours matching in this palette is a coincidence of this
     * palette, not a constraint — collapsing them would rebuild that coupling
     * under a different name.
     *
     * POLARITY FOLLOWS THE SURFACE. The filled region is the figure and the
     * track is the ground, so --progress-fill flips with the theme (deep on
     * light, pale on dark) while --progress-track stays a quiet channel about
     * 1.5:1 from its card. Do NOT "improve" the bar by darkening the track in
     * light mode: that makes the *unfilled* remainder the strongest mark on the
     * card and inverts what the bar reports.
     *
     * --on-brand is the ink for content sitting ON --progress-fill, so it flips
     * with the fill. It is not a fixed dark: a dark icon on the light-mode fill
     * would be 2.77:1, the same class of failure it was introduced to fix.
     */
    :root[data-theme='light'] {
        --background: #FAFAFA; /* grey-50 */
        --card-background: #F5F5F5; /* grey-100 */
        --card-border: #E5E5E5; /* grey-200 */
        --shadow: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --accent: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --text: #0B0B0B; /* darkslate-900 */
        --progress-fill: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --progress-track: #E3B3B8; /* primary-150 */
        --status-live: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --status-halo: #A82334; /* primary-600 */
        --brand-ink: #A82334; /* primary-600 — 6.52:1 on the card */
        --on-brand: #FAFAFA; /* grey-50 — light ink, the fill is deep here */
    }

    :root[data-theme='dark'] {
        --background: #111111; /* darkslate-700 */
        --card-background: #171717; /* darkslate-500 */
        --card-border: #2C2C2C; /* darkslate-100 */
        --shadow: #F3A3AA; /* primary-200 */
        --accent: #F9CDD3; /* primary-100 */
        --text: #FAFAFA; /* grey-50 */
        --progress-fill: #F9CDD3; /* primary-100 */
        --progress-track: #462F32; /* primary-800 */
        --status-live: #F3A3AA; /* primary-200 */
        --status-halo: #F9CDD3; /* primary-100 */
        --brand-ink: #F3A3AA; /* primary-200 — 9.08:1 on the card */
        --on-brand: #0B0B0B; /* darkslate-900 — dark ink, the fill is pale here */
    }

    /* THE CONTROL ROW WRAPS, AND IT DOES NOT COUNT. Three hand-written width
       queries used to grant it 4/3/2/1 columns; they are deleted, and what
       replaces them is one declaration that cannot be tuned wrong.

       WHY THE LADDER WENT. A column count is an approximation of "how many fit",
       maintained by hand against bounds that had to be kept in step with the
       breakpoints by hand, and it approximated wrong twice in a row — first
       stopping at two columns, then needing a fourth rung at a bound (13rem) that
       took a fitted budget to derive. The mechanism is worth restating because it
       is the reason a count can never be right here: two controls plus their
       separation are a fixed 9rem, since the control's box is text-relative, but A
       CARD'S WIDTH IS NOT — a card grows vertically with its content and never
       horizontally, because it is as wide as the viewport allows. So past some
       reader text size any fixed count exceeds the card, this row held the intro
       card's copy column open at its own minimum width, and the card's clipping
       sheared the hero copy and the controls both.

       WHAT REPLACES IT IS AN INVARIANT, not a smaller number. A wrapping row's
       minimum content width is its LARGEST ITEM, not the sum of a row of them —
       every item here is one declared box, so that minimum is exactly one control
       at every viewport and every text size. The row can therefore never hold the
       copy column wider than one control, which is the defect class rather than an
       instance of it, and there is no bound left to keep in step with anything.

       TWO THINGS ARE NOW LOAD-BEARING that were not while this was a two-dimensional
       layout, and both are asserted in tests/control-geometry.test.ts rather than
       left to hold by luck. The control's own `shrink-0` stops the box being
       compressed below its declared width, which the ladder made irrelevant and
       this makes decisive again (uno.config.ts has the history — it once measured a
       control at 47.80px). And every child of this row has to BE a control: one
       child with a wider minimum width would restore exactly the defect above, so
       the row's minimum is only one control for as long as that holds.

       MEASURED, against the ladder it replaces rather than against the defect both
       of them fix. Text ink past a card's right clip edge and control boxes past it
       are 0 at every one of the 32 configurations either was measured at, with the
       one documented residual unchanged: at 320 wide and a 40px root one
       unbreakable hero word still overruns by 34.58, which is this card's own
       padding eating 120 of 320 and is not this row's doing. Deleting the rungs
       also removes the last two rem bounds that had no variant sibling to move with.
       What the ladder's absence used to cost — the text figures, the control-box
       figures, and the two different onset text sizes they imply — is recorded once,
       in uno.config.ts's note on the control box, rather than restated here.

       IT CHANGES THE DEFAULT TEXT SIZE, which the ladder deliberately did not, and
       that is a design change carried on purpose rather than a side effect: the row
       now fits as many controls as there is room for instead of as many as a rung
       allows. At a 16px root, 360 and 375 wide go from 2 per row over 4 rows to 3
       over 3 (document height 1856 -> 1792), 414 goes from 3 to 4, and 320 is
       unchanged at 2. Wider viewports gain more, because the old top rung capped
       every one of them at 4: at 1024 and a 24px root all seven now sit on one row.
       14 of the swept configurations get shorter, by up to 160px.

       TWO GET TALLER, and the reason is the whole trade rather than a wrinkle in it.
       At 320 and 360 wide at a 24px root the row drops to one control per row where
       the ladder seated two, and the page grows 372px and 330px. Measured, because
       the mechanism is not what it looks like: the ladder was not fitting two
       controls into the room available, it was DEMANDING room for two and getting
       it. Its minimum width of 216 held the copy column open to 216, and at that
       text size 216 still landed 42px inside the card's clip edge, so it fitted. A
       wrapping row demands nothing, so the copy column shrinks to the 174 actually
       on offer and two controls no longer fit it.

       That demand is not separable into a good half and a bad half — it is one
       behaviour. The same "hold the column open to N controls regardless of the
       room" that won 372px of height here is exactly what sheared 142px of BUTTON
       at 320 and a 40px root. Trading two configurations that grow and stay
       scrollable for a class of configurations that silently deleted functionality
       is the trade, stated plainly, and it is why this is the better engineering
       rather than merely the tidier CSS.

       Worth knowing for whoever wants that height back: below `md` the portrait is
       positioned out of flow, but its wrapper is still an in-flow sibling of the
       copy column and spends 24 of the card's 198 usable pixels there. Recovering
       those would seat two controls at both of the configurations above. That is a
       fix to the mobile hero, not to this row, and it is deliberately not made here.

       THE HONEST LIMIT IS UNCHANGED, because it never was the ladder's to fix: once
       ONE control plus the chrome between it and the viewport exceeds that viewport,
       the control itself is clipped and no layout here can help. That happens beyond
       roughly a 48px root on a 320px phone — past 300% zoom, comfortably clear of
       the 200% SC 1.4.4 asks for — and the test asserts that margin as a number so
       it cannot rot. Fixing it needs a control that shrinks or a card that scrolls
       sideways.

       ONE WHOLE CLASS OF CHANGE HERE STOPPED BEING DANGEROUS, which is the clearest
       single argument for this shape. The separation below used to be able to delete
       functionality: widened to 6rem under the column layout it pushed a control past
       the card's clip edge at 12 of the 32 measured configurations, worst 89px, and
       12px of it AT THE DEFAULT TEXT SIZE. The same widening was built against this
       row and measures 0 at all 32, because a separation only applies between items
       that share a line and the minimum here is one item on a line by itself. So it
       can make this row taller and never wider, and the test that used to have to
       re-resolve it at every width no longer does.

       `justify-content` is deliberately absent: a wrapping row packs its items at
       the start of the line already, so declaring it restated the default. The
       ladder needed it to stop content-sized tracks stretching. */
    .control-row {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: 1rem; /* separates the controls from each other, on both axes */
    }

    /* Card entrance — replaces the JS stagger. Pure CSS, no client bundle.
       One rule per direct child of <main>: when a card is added, extend this
       ladder (a build-output test compares the ladder to the child count). */
    main > * {
        animation: card-in 0.4s ease-out backwards;
    }

    main > *:nth-child(1) { animation-delay: 0.00s; }
    main > *:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: 0.08s; }
    main > *:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: 0.16s; }
    main > *:nth-child(4) { animation-delay: 0.24s; }
    main > *:nth-child(5) { animation-delay: 0.32s; }
    main > *:nth-child(6) { animation-delay: 0.40s; }
    main > *:nth-child(7) { animation-delay: 0.48s; }
    main > *:nth-child(8) { animation-delay: 0.56s; }

    @keyframes card-in {
        from {
            opacity: 0;
            transform: translateY(40%);
        }
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        main > * {
            animation: none;
        }
    }
</style>
